### Viewer widgets

- `TextView`
- `TextArea` / `TextEditor`
- `MarkdownView`
- `SyntaxView`
- `HexView`

`HexView.from_file(path)` reads only the visible part of a file instead of loading the complete file into memory. Viewer widgets now use a black content background by default, independently of the surrounding application theme.

`SyntaxView` detects a lexer from the filename/extension via Pygments and defaults to the `vim` syntax colour theme. This includes Markdown and HTML source highlighting as well as common programming/configuration formats.

`MarkdownView` preserves Rich styling and uses the Vim Pygments theme for fenced and inline code by default. `TextView`, `SyntaxView`, and `HexView` expose a horizontal viewport so long text/source/binary rows are not truncated. `MarkdownView(wrap=False)` enables the same horizontal viewport for unwrapped Markdown rendering.

### Menus and command surfaces

- `CommandWindow` - dBASE/FoxPro-style command history + editable prompt

- `MenuBar`
- `MenuDesktop` - keeps the bar at one row and composites dropdown menus over the client area
- `Menu`
- `MenuItem`
- `Separator`
- nested submenus
- checked/radio menu entries
- `ContextMenu`
- `FunctionBar`
- `StatusBar`

### File-selection dialogs

- `FileDialog`
- `DirectoryDialog`

## Modal compositing and positioned windows

`Application.push_modal()` keeps the current screen behind a `Dialog` and composites the modal instead of replacing the complete screen. Dialogs still center by default, and the 0.5 line permits explicit terminal-cell positioning, a DOS-style shadow, and a palette-backed color scheme:

```python
dialog = Dialog(
    content,
    title="Confirmation",
    width=48,
    height=9,
    top=4,
    left=10,
    shadow=True,
    panel=True,
    color_scheme=5,
);
app.push_modal(dialog);
```

`top` and `left` are zero-based terminal-cell coordinates. Leaving either unset preserves centered behavior. The generic primitive is intentionally language-neutral; sumX `DEFINE WINDOW ... FROM ... TO ... SHADOW PANEL COLOR SCHEME n` maps onto it rather than implementing a second window system.

Viewer-style dialogs can force the complete interior to the viewer palette (black by default):

```python
Dialog(viewer, title="View", maximizable=True, content_style="viewer");
```

Close the current modal with:

```python
app.pop_modal();
```

Nested modal levels are supported. Dialogs may opt in to maximize/restore support with `maximizable=True`; F11 toggles between the configured dialog geometry and the full terminal window. See `examples/demo_positioned_dialog.py`.

## Menu navigation

`MenuDesktop(menu, body)` is the recommended host for full-screen editor/IDE applications. The menu bar remains one row high; active dropdowns are composited over the client area rather than consuming layout height, so an editor panel cannot paint over the menu and opening a menu does not resize the document viewport. See `examples/demo_menu_desktop.py`.


The intended Commander/DOS behaviour is:

```text
F9          open/close menu
Left/Right  previous/next top-level menu, or enter/leave submenu
Up/Down     previous/next item
Enter       activate
Esc         close submenu/menu
```

Applications may bind F9 to place focus on the menu bar and call `open()`.

## Form navigation

```text
Tab / Shift+Tab    next / previous control
Space / Enter      buttons and checkboxes
Arrow keys         radio groups, choices and sliders
Home / End         first/last or minimum/maximum when applicable
PageUp / PageDown  larger navigation step where applicable
```

Radio buttons use arrows to move and select the previous/next choice. Consecutive checkboxes use arrows to move focus without changing their checked state; Space/Enter toggles the current checkbox.

## Viewer navigation

`TextView`, `MarkdownView`, `SyntaxView`, `HexView`, `TableView`, `ListView`, and `TreeView` use familiar navigation keys:

```text
Up / Down                  vertical line
PageUp / PageDown          vertical page
Home / End                 top / bottom
Left / Right               horizontal cell
Shift+Left / Shift+Right   horizontal page
Ctrl+Left / Ctrl+Right     left / right edge
```

`TextView`, `SyntaxView`, and `HexView` support horizontal scrolling. `MarkdownView` keeps normal wrapped rendering by default; construct it with `wrap=False` for a horizontally scrollable unwrapped viewport. `HexView` displays offset, hexadecimal bytes and ASCII text side by side.

