## CommandWindow

`CommandWindow` is intended for interactive language shells such as sumX. It provides an editable prompt, command history with Up/Down, cursor editing, Ctrl+L clearing, styled output, and a black command area. It also has a persistent zero-based coordinate screen layer for classic terminal-language output such as xBase `@ row,col SAY`: `write_at(row, column, text)`.

The command window has its own output scrollback. `PageUp` moves one page toward older output and `PageDown` returns toward the live tail. `Shift+PageUp` / `Shift+PageDown` are accepted as aliases when the terminal forwards them. Many Linux terminal emulators reserve the Shift+Page shortcuts for their own scrollback and do not deliver them to full-screen applications, so unmodified PageUp/PageDown are the portable in-application keys. Typing or normal cursor/history editing returns to the live prompt. This is application scrollback, independent of the terminal emulator's own scrollback.

When a command shell lives inside a `Panel`, use `Panel(command, content_style="command")` so the whole panel interior, including padding and unused rows, uses the command background.

```python
from sumtui import CommandWindow;

cmd = CommandWindow(prompt=". ", on_submit=lambda text, window: window.write(text));
```

Run the demo:

```bash
python examples/demo_commandwindow.py
```


## Absolute-position GET fields

`CommandWindow` can host editable fields at absolute row/column positions. `ScreenField.width` and `.height` are **viewport dimensions**, while `max_length` is an independent logical data limit. A narrow one-line field scrolls horizontally over a longer value; a field with `height > 1` uses a multiline, soft-wrapped viewport with vertical scrolling. `HEIGHT` therefore does not silently truncate data.

`define_field()` and `begin_read()` provide the low-level screen-form layer used by sumX `@ ... GET` and `READ`. In multiline fields Enter creates a real newline. Tab advances to the next field and Tab on the final field accepts the READ; Ctrl+Enter remains an optional accept alias when a terminal can report it distinctly. `commit_screen_to_history()` archives the finished coordinate/GET layer as ordinary scrollable text and removes its live field styling.

## Data forms

`sumTUI 0.5.3` includes reusable data-oriented forms intended for xBase/database applications:

```python
RecordForm([
    FormField("name", width=20, mask="X" * 20),
    FormField("amount", width=7, mask="9990.00"),
])

BrowseForm(["id", "name", "amount"], rows)
```

`RecordForm` represents one record at a time, while `BrowseForm` renders a tabular record browser with field names as column headers and a `Rec n/N` status line. `TextInput(mask=...)` keeps a field picture visible while the value is empty.
 `RecordForm.set_values()` reloads a record in place; `BrowseForm` provides `first()`, `previous()`, `next()`, `last()`, and `find()` helpers for database-style navigation bars.

### RecordForm data entry

`RecordForm` is editable by default for every non-read-only field.  `Enter`, `Down`, and `Tab` advance through fields; `Up` and `Shift+Tab` move backward.  Host applications can bind `Ctrl+End` for save and `Esc` for abort without the field editor swallowing those keys.

## Generic editor and text-file tools

`sumedit FILE` opens a lightweight plain-text editor built from sumTUI widgets. It supports keyboard selection, system/internal clipboard integration, undo/redo, word navigation, document-edge navigation, scrollbars, encoding/EOL status, search/replace, semantic syntax highlighting, and optional visualization of spaces, tabs, line endings and control characters. **Ctrl+Home** jumps to the beginning of the document and **Ctrl+End** to the end. **Shift+Up/Down** and **Shift+PageUp/PageDown** extend the current selection while moving vertically or by a page. Its top `File / Edit / Search / View / Options / Help` menu is always visible; **F9** opens the menu and **F10** exits. Dropdowns overlay the editing panel rather than being clipped by it. **F1** opens editor help in a modal dialog, and `Help -> About...` shows version/license information.

Syntax highlighting is enabled by default and uses `Auto` detection from filename/extension, exact names such as `README`, and shebangs where useful. `View -> Syntax highlighting` can disable it without changing the file, while `View -> Syntax` can override the detected language. The editor maps lexer-specific tokens onto stable semantic roles, so a keyword, string, number, comment, function, type, variable, operator, and error keep the same visual meaning across languages even when the selected theme changes. Built-in modes include Markdown, sumX, xBase/FoxPro, Python, Bash/shell, C/C++, R, Ruby, BASIC, Java, PHP, SQL, HTML, JavaScript, VBScript, CSS, JSON, YAML, TOML, INI/config, XML, and generic logs.

Markdown is treated as editable source rather than rendered documentation. Headings, emphasis, links, inline HTML, and fenced blocks are highlighted; fenced blocks such as `python`, `bash`, `sql`, `basic`, `sumx`, and other known languages reuse the same semantic colours they receive when opened directly. In particular, the project closing line remains visible/editable as HTML source:

```html
<p align=center><b>- oOo -</b></p>
```

A rendered Markdown/document preview and possible sumDOC integration are intentionally **not** part of this lightweight editor stage; those remain suitable for a future optional `sumedit advanced`.

`View` entries are checked menu items and may be toggled with **Space** while the menu remains open. Hidden text can be distinguished visually: spaces use `·`, tabs use `⇥`, LF line endings use `↵`, CRLF uses `⏎`, classic Mac CR uses `↩`, and control codes use Unicode control pictures such as `␀` / `␡` with a separate control-code style. Binary-looking files still require the explicit `--force` switch before opening as text.

`Search` provides `Find...` (**Ctrl+F**), `Find Next` (**F3**), `Find Previous` (**Shift+F3**), `Search & Replace...` (**Ctrl+H**), and `Go to Line...` (**Ctrl+G**). Search is literal and case-insensitive by default; the modal dialog can enable case-sensitive, whole-word, regular-expression, and wrap-around matching. Replace supports one-match replacement and Replace All, with the resulting edit kept in the normal undo history. File Open uses **Ctrl+O**, leaving F3 consistently available for repeated search.

On POSIX terminals, `sumedit` temporarily captures control keys rather than letting the terminal driver convert them to process-control signals. This is what makes **Ctrl+C = Copy**, **Ctrl+Z = Undo**, **Ctrl+Y = Redo**, and **Ctrl+S = Save** reliable inside the editor. The implementation disables `ISIG` and XON/XOFF only while the application owns the terminal and restores the original termios settings in a `finally`-equivalent context on exit. Other sumTUI applications keep the traditional signal behavior unless they explicitly request `Application(capture_control_keys=True)`.

`Options` contains nested `Tab -> 2/4/8`, `Theme -> ...`, `Line wrapping -> ...`, and `Line breaking -> ...` menus plus `Keyboard shortcuts...`. `Theme -> Ralesk's MC` reproduces the comfortable Geany/MC-inspired palette supplied with the project; on a fresh configuration it is selected by default.  The shortcut editor works with named actions rather than hard-coded keys: select an action, then Change, Add, or Remove one of its shortcuts, or restore all Defaults.  The next real terminal key combination is captured, conflicts in the same editor context are reported before reassignment, and actions may keep more than one spelling (for example `Ctrl+C` and `Ctrl+Insert` for Copy).  Menus and the bottom function bar are regenerated from the active bindings, so their shortcut hints stay accurate after customization.

`line_wrapping` is presentation-only and follows a compact Spectrum-style convention: `-1` means **Auto** (the default, wrap at the current visible editor width), `0` means **Off**, and any positive value is a fixed maximum visual width.  Presets include **78**, **80**, **100**, and **120** columns.  The 78-column preset is intentionally retained for legacy 80-column layouts: one border cell on each side of an 80-column window leaves 78 useful text columns.  Soft wrapping never inserts an EOL, never marks the file modified, resets horizontal scrolling, and uses `↪` in the line-number gutter for visual continuation rows.

`line_breaking` is separate because it changes the document.  It is **Off (`0`) by default**; positive presets such as 78/80/100/120 enable hard breaking while typing when the current logical line exceeds the selected width.  The inserted break is part of normal editor undo history.  This distinction lets source/log files use visual wrapping safely while prose can opt into physical line breaks deliberately.

`Options -> Save configuration` stores the current theme, tab width, line-wrapping/line-breaking choices, hidden-character visibility choices, syntax-highlighting state/mode, and only the keyboard overrides that differ from the built-in defaults in `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sumtui/edit.json` (or `~/.config/sumtui/edit.json`). The configuration is loaded on the next start; `sumedit --theme NAME` overrides the saved theme for that invocation.  The reusable `KeyBindingManager` is also public API for other sumTUI applications, with named actions, multiple bindings, contexts, conflict detection and default restoration.

To install the short `edit` wrapper under your own `$HOME/bin` without touching `/usr/bin/edit`:

```bash
sumedit --install-alias
```

The wrapper deliberately uses `exec python3 -m sumtui.tools.edit "$@"` so quoting and argument boundaries are preserved. To force the Geany-inspired palette for one invocation, use `sumedit --theme "Ralesk's MC" FILE`; `examples/bash/edit_ralesk.sh` demonstrates the same from Bash.

A runnable Python example for hidden-character rendering is included as:

```bash
python examples/demo_editor_hidden_chars.py
python examples/demo_editor_syntax.py
python examples/demo_editor_wrapping.py
python examples/demo_ralesk_theme.py
```

Python and Bash examples also cover the reusable key-binding registry and a temporary custom `sumedit` keyboard profile without touching the user's real configuration:

```bash
python examples/demo_control_keys.py
python examples/demo_keybindings.py
bash examples/bash/edit_file.sh README.md
bash examples/bash/edit_custom_keys.sh README.md
bash examples/bash/edit_markdown.sh README.md
bash examples/bash/edit_wrapping.sh README.md
```

`sumeol --check FILE` reports encoding and LF/CRLF/CR usage. Conversions use `--to lf`, `--to crlf`, or `--to cr`, preserving the detected character encoding by default. `sumeol --install-compat-aliases` optionally installs `dos2unix`, `unix2dos`, `mac2unix`, and `unix2mac` wrappers in `$HOME/bin`.


## suminput: terminal input for shell scripts

`suminput` is a small reusable input tool built on the same sumTUI controls.  Its terminal UI is written to the controlling terminal while **stdout is reserved for the resulting value**, so command substitution works cleanly:

```bash
name=$(suminput "What is your name? ")
password=$(suminput --hidden "Password: ")
pin=$(suminput --mask "***" --width 8 "PIN: ")
phone=$(suminput --picture "(999) 999-9999" --width 14 "Phone: ")
notes=$(suminput --dialog --width 50 --height 6 "Notes")
```

`--mask` repeats the supplied visual string once per entered character; the real value is never copied to the display.  `--hidden` uses no echo at all.  Secret input is not placed in editor/command history or the system clipboard by `suminput`.

DOS/4DOS-style choice behavior is available without requiring a separate `choice` utility:

```bash
answer=$(suminput --keys YN --default N --timeout N,10 --dialog "Continue?")
answer=$(suminput -c:YN -t:N,10 "Continue?")
```

`--case-sensitive` / `-s` makes key matching exact.  Timeout exit status is `3`; the selected/default value is still written to stdout, so scripts can distinguish an explicit answer from a timeout if they need to.

A shell-safe assignment can also be emitted:

```bash
eval "$(suminput --variable username 'User: ')"
# Equivalent old-style spelling is accepted: -v:username
```

Normal command substitution is preferred when possible because it avoids `eval`:

```bash
username=$(suminput "User: ")
```

`--width`, `--height`, and `--picture` mirror the input concepts used by sumX GET fields.  `HEIGHT > 1` creates a multiline dialog area.  PICTURE masking and secret echo modes are currently single-line, which keeps their cursor/mask semantics deterministic.

When `--dialog` is used as a standalone shell command, sumTUI uses the terminal's alternate screen so the previous shell screen is restored exactly when the dialog disappears.  When the same dialog components are used inside an existing sumTUI application, they composite over the live application background.

Exit status conventions are:

```text
0  accepted
1  cancelled / Escape
2  command-line usage error
3  timeout
4  controlling-terminal error
```

Runnable shell examples are included under `examples/bash/`:

```bash
bash examples/bash/suminput_choice.sh
bash examples/bash/eol_convert.sh README.md
```

The equivalent Python-side positioned-window example is `examples/demo_positioned_dialog.py`. This keeps the documentation practical across Python and shell usage rather than leaving cross-environment behavior as prose only.

## Small tools, fewer mandatory external utilities

sumTUI intentionally exposes small reusable capabilities as tools when the implementation already exists inside the toolkit.  `sumedit`, `sumeol`, `suminput`, and `sumtheme` are examples: the goal is not to replace every Unix utility, but to keep basic educational/editor/input workflows usable without making unrelated external packages mandatory.  Optional system integrations may improve behavior, while the core path remains available from sumTUI itself.

## Theme editor and user themes

`sumtheme` opens the generic sumTUI theme editor. Built-in themes are intentionally read-only: clone one first, then edit semantic roles such as `syntax_keyword`, `syntax_string`, `editor_gutter`, `menu`, `selection`, `dialog`, and `status` while a live preview uses the same theme engine as normal applications.

```bash
sumtheme
sumtheme --theme "Ralesk's MC"
sumtheme --list
sumtheme --dir
```

User themes are stored as JSON under `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sumtui/themes` (or `~/.config/sumtui/themes`). They are discovered automatically on later starts and appear in `sumedit -> Options -> Theme` and in other clients of the public theme registry. The public `theme_to_dict()` / `theme_from_dict()` APIs serialize an **effective** theme, which is useful when an application must carry its palette without depending on the user's local theme files.

```python
from sumtui import make_theme, save_user_theme, theme_to_dict;

theme = make_theme("Ralesk's MC").copy(name="Teaching MC");
path = save_user_theme(theme);
embedded = theme_to_dict(theme);
```

See `examples/demo_theme_serialization.py` and `examples/bash/theme_editor.sh`.


